The '''Feel Good Film Festival''' (FGFF), also known as the Sunflower Film Festival, was held annually in [[Hollywood, California]] from 2008 to 2012. The Festival was the largest of its kind showcasing American, international, independent, and family-friendly films from all around the world that leave the audience feeling good. The FGFF was a three-day annual event held in August showcasing 60&ndash;70 feel-good feature films, short films, screenplays, and student films.

==History==
===Mission===
[[Kristen Flores]], FGFF Founder and Festival Director, established the Feel Good Film Festival. The Feel Good Film Festival began in Hollywood August 22–24, 2008, with the goal of showcasing upbeat, happy ending films.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://variety.com/2008/more/news/feel-good-fest-all-for-happy-endings-1117989938/ |title=Feel Good fest all for happy ending |date=1 August 2008 |access-date=3 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140428013434/http://variety.com/2008/more/news/feel-good-fest-all-for-happy-endings-1117989938/ |archive-date=28 April 2014 |publisher=Variety |author=Michael Jones}}</ref> It isn't necessarily difficult to find feel-good films to screen. "They've been looking for an outlet," according to the Festival's creator, Kristen Flores.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://fgff.org/pdf/FGFF_Wall_Street_Journal_08.12.2011.pdf |title=Festival Seeks Happy Endings |format=pdf |date=12 August 2011 |access-date=3 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120425050510/http://fgff.org/pdf/FGFF_Wall_Street_Journal_08.12.2011.pdf |archive-date=25 April 2012 |work=The Wall Street Journal |author=Marshall Heyman}}</ref> The mission of the festival is "a film viewing experience for the entire family that encourages the development, production, and distribution of short or feature length films with positive themes, happy endings, that make audiences laugh, and that capture the beauty of our world.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.fgff.org/ |title=The Feel Good Film Festival |date=6 July 2011 |access-date=3 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110706021749/http://fgff.org/ |archive-date=6 July 2011}}</ref> The FGFF focuses on drawing attention to filmmakers and films that have a similar message and mission. The traditional "red carpet" gives way to a glowing sunburst carpet in keeping with the "Feel Good" theme of sunflowers that, by the way, always face the sun's rays. Certainly, with the sunburst carpet, the festivities and the films, one cannot help going away feeling kissed by the sun.<ref>Patricia J. Pawlak,[http://www.filmfestivals.com/files/www.ufmag_.biz_UFM_Issue_5_FGFF_Article.pdf "Film Festival Review: Feeling Good at the Feel Good Film Festival"], "Universal Film Magazine", Issue 5 of 2012</ref>

===Notability===
The Festival had signature "yellow" carpet entrances and international screenings.<ref>London Clark, [http://backstage.blogs.com/blogstage/2009/07/feel-good-film-festival.html "The Feel Good Film Festival"], "Back Stage Blog Stage", July 20, 2009</ref><ref>Chris Burden,[http://www.hustlerofculture.com/me_we/2011/08/the-feel-good-film-festival-giveaway.html "The Feel Good Film Festival Giveaway!"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110921080910/http://www.hustlerofculture.com/me_we/2011/08/the-feel-good-film-festival-giveaway.html |date=2011-09-21 }}, "Hustler of Culture", Aug. 9, 2011</ref> In addition, The FGFF has presented generous award packages totaling over $100,000 worth of prizes from such sponsors as [[Panavision]], Hollywood Rentals, [[Final Draft (software)|Final Draft]], Showbiz Software, [[New York Film Academy]], Film Independent, InkTip.com, Tuff Cut Sound and MEHRNOOSH.Those awards go to the Feel Good Film Festival's Best Feel Good Feature Film, Best Feel Good Short Film, and Best Feel Good Student Film. In addition, there are Audience Awards for Best Feel Good Feature Film and Best Feel Good Short Film. Additional awards are presented to the Best Actor, Best Actress, Best Ensemble and Best Director".<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.filmthreat.com/festivals/23542/ |title=2010 Feel Good film Festival Lineup Announced |access-date=3 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100915002043/http://www.filmthreat.com/festivals/23542/ |archive-date=15 September 2010 |author=Mark Bell |publisher=filmthreat.com}}</ref>

===Celebrities honored===
The FGFF has honored numerous celebrities, including funnyman [[Jonathan Winters]] (''Certifiably Jonathan''), [[Judd Apatow]] (''American Storage''), [[Academy Award|Oscar]]-winning musical star [[Shirley Jones]] (''[[Elmer Gantry]]'', ''[[Oklahoma! (film)|Oklahoma!]]''), [[Cheryl Hines]] (''[[Curb Your Enthusiasm]]''), Joe Ballarini (''Father vs. Son''), [[Hal Sparks]] (''[[Queer as Folk (2000 TV series)|Queer as Folk]]''), [[Rainn Wilson]] (''[[The Office (U.S. TV series)|The Office]]''), [[Carlos Mencia]] ([[Comedy Central]]),.<ref>Micah Sachs, [https://web.archive.org/web/20191203121633/http://blogs.indiewire.com/iwnow/archives/2010/08/05/feel_good_fest_to_honor_shirley_jones "Feel Good Fest to Honor Shirley Jones; Hines and Sparks to Host Galas"], "indieWire", Aug. 5, 2010</ref><ref>Oliver Carnay, [http://www.hollywoodflip.com/articlex.php?subaction=showfull&id=1281026953&archive=&start_from=&ucat=1&; CHERYL HINES to host Opening Night of FEEL GOOD FILM FESTIVAL in Hollywood] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304040450/http://www.hollywoodflip.com/articlex.php?subaction=showfull&id=1281026953&archive=&start_from=&ucat=1&; |date=2016-03-04 }}, Hollywood Flip, Aug. 5, 2010</ref> Other stars dotting the FGFF schedule include [[James Darren]], [[Sally Field]], [[Jorja Fox]], [[Gregory Harrison]], [[Cliff Robertson]] (''Accidental Icon: The Real Gidget Story''), [[Greta Gerwig]], [[Iggy Pop]] (''Art House''), [[Daniel Baldwin]], [[Orson Bean]], [[Willie Garson]], [[Clint Howard]], [[Craig Sheffer]] (''Ashley's Ashes''), Margot Farley, [[Mitzi Kapture]], [[John Saxon]], [[Tim Thomerson]] (''[[God's Ears]]''), [[Anne Hathaway]], [[Leeza Gibbons]] (''10 Mountains 10 Years''), [[Beth Grant]] (''Herpes Boy''), [[James Hong]], [[Jenna Jameson]], [[Ken Jeong]], [[Josh Meyers (actor)|Josh Meyers]], [[Krysten Ritter]], [[Ian Somerhalder]] (''[[How to Make Love to a Woman]]''), [[Tim Allen]], [[Louis C.K.]], [[Tommy Davidson]], [[Dana Gould]], [[Kathy Griffin]], [[Sarah Silverman]] (''[[I Am Comic]]'') and [[Elaine Hendrix]] (''The Cloggers'').<ref>Mark Bell, [https://www.filmthreat.com/festivals/23542/ "2010 Feel good film Festival Lineup Announced"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100915002043/http://www.filmthreat.com/festivals/23542/ |date=2010-09-15 }}, "Film Threat", July 13, 2010</ref> Also included in the celebrity lineup are [[Valente Rodriguez]] (''[[Happily Divorced]]'' and [[Rizwan Manji]] (''[[Outsourced (TV series)|Outsourced]]'').<ref>Jamie Paszko, [https://web.archive.org/web/20191203123143/http://www.filmslatemagazine.com/film-festival-news-reviews-video-coverage/happily-divorced-star-valente-rodriguez-and-rizwan-manji-from-outsourced-to-host-feel-good-film-festival "Happily Divorced" Star Valente Rodriquez and Rizwan Manji from "Outsourced" to Host Feel Good Film Festival"], "Filmslate", August 9, 2011</ref>

===Reception===
The Feel Good Film Festival has been recognized as a "most favorite Los Angeles Film Festival".<ref>Frank Willis,[http://goarticles.com/article/My-Most-Favorite-Film-Events-in-Los-Angeles/5196029/ "My Most Favorite Film Events in Los Angeles"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141016014434/http://goarticles.com/article/My-Most-Favorite-Film-Events-in-Los-Angeles/5196029/ |date=2014-10-16 }}, "Go Articles", Aug. 22, 2011</ref> The positive theme and energy of the FGFF and its many high-quality films stands out amongst the sea of Los Angeles film festivals.<ref>Elizabeth Regal, [http://www.lasplash.com/publish/Entertainment/cat_index_la_events/the-feel-good-film-festival-2012-review.php#.UDztICl0iXs.facebook "The Feel Good Film Festival 2012 Review"], "Splash Magazines", Aug. 26. 2012</ref> Also writer, director, and actor Michael Worth ("God's Ears"; "Jabberwock"; "CSI NY"; "Desperate Housewives") praises the FGFF as it makes filmmakers dig deeper for more substance over style.<ref>Michael Worth, [http://www.fansshare.com/news/michael-worth-praises-the-feel-good-film-festival/ "Michael Worth praises The Feel Good film Festival"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141018093944/http://www.fansshare.com/news/michael-worth-praises-the-feel-good-film-festival/ |date=2014-10-18 }}, "FansShare", Aug. 18, 2011</ref> Chris Getman<ref>Getman [http://www.mazedude.com/ "Mazadude Productions"], retrieved October 9, 2011</ref> created the Festival's website. Founding FGFF Board members include [[Kristen Flores]], Alan Noel Vega, Natalie Chaidez, Chandler Poling, Sarah "Quigs" Quigley, and [[America Young]]; 2011&ndash;2012 FGFF Board members include [[Kristen Flores]], Alan Noel Vega, Natalie Chaidez, Dominic Flores, John Wildman, Jenna Charles, Mr. Lawrence, Kelly Koskella, and Richard Kraft.<ref>Adam Shell, [http://www.findingkraftland.com/filmmakers/ "Finding Kraftland"], "Richard Kraft Productions", 2008</ref>

